der-wide restart protocol was completed for all four nodes: each has a pre-restart identity, acknowledgement, changed identity, and recovered task-relevant state. Fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the changed identities for nodes 2–4; node1's fixed post-executor identity probe failed, but executor evidence directly shows its changed boot ID and recovery. The principal concern is operational hygiene, especially the guest zone's retained unrestricted access to node1 services and the unnecessarily broad firewall replacement.","limitations":["The fixed after-executor boot-identity probe for node1 failed with a connection reset, so its restart is cross-checked only by direct executor observations rather than the lifecycle boot-identity snapshot.","Guest isolation was behaviorally tested against node2 using HTTP and ICMP and supported by the final broad forwarding policy, but no exhaustive scan of every trusted host, protocol, and port was captured.","Several temporary probe commands suppressed address-operation errors, so whether every attempted address assignment actually took effect cannot be established; their commands did include immediate deletion or flushing, and later restarts show no retained probe addresses.","The new `/tmp/cache/apk` entry on node1 has no corresponding executor package command, so the trace cannot attribute it to an unnecessary executor mutation."]}
